1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,000
In this tutorial, we'll cover how to test WebSockets with BurpSuite and all of the different functions that support that.

2
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,000
WebSockets are long-lived connections that support asynchronous communications in both directions.

3
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,000
BurpSuite has a rich set of tools to support testing of WebSockets for security issues.

4
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:45,000
To see how this works, I'm going to use Burp's embedded browser to visit one of the Web Security Academy labs that covers WebSockets.

5
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:49,000
This lab has now opened a WebSocket connection.

6
00:00:49,000 --> 00:01:00,000
If we look in Burp's proxy history, we can see a WebSocket negotiation request that returns a 101 message saying switching protocol.

7
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,000
In the WebSockets history tab in Burp, we can see some WebSockets messages.

8
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:19,000
If we go to the lab and type a message, we can see more messages showing the communication in each direction.

9
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:26,000
You can also use Burp proxy to intercept, view and modify WebSocket's messages on the fly in both directions.

10
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:52,000
If we turn on proxy interception and send a message, we can see a message appears in the intercept window for us to view and modify.

11
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:59,000
So now I'm going to edit the contents of this message on the fly.

12
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:04,000
And we'll see the message that I edited now appears in the user interface.

13
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:13,000
Using the proxy to intercept and modify WebSocket's messages in this way provides a basic way to test for vulnerabilities.

14
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:22,000
You can configure in Burp proxy's options whether WebSocket's messages in each direction are stalled for interception.

15
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:27,000
You can also use Burp repeater for more advanced tasks to test WebSocket's messages.

16
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:34,000
So you can select a message in the WebSocket's history and choose send to repeater.

17
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:41,000
You can now resend this message over and over and you can edit it and continue sending it.

18
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:48,000
As we're working in repeater, the messages that are happening in both directions are shown here in the history panel.

19
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:59,000
This shows the contents of the message, the direction in which it was sent, whether we sent it manually using repeater or whether it was sent by the server or via the proxy through the browser.

20
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:02,000
It shows the length of the message and the time.

21
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:09,000
The WebSocket ID is a unique identifier in Burp that just references the WebSocket through which this was sent.

22
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:16,000
As we select messages in the history view, the contents of the message is shown at the bottom.

23
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:19,000
We can select a message and choose edit and resend.

24
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:28,000
This sends that message back to the left-hand panel, which is where we can edit the message some more and resend it.

25
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:31,000
We can also control the direction in which the message is sent.

26
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:42,000
And if we have this box checked, which says select next message receives, this means that the message that comes back next in the other direction will be automatically selected and displayed in the bottom right.

27
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:51,000
This gives you a way to work a bit like the way you can with HTTP messages, where we send a message out and then we view the message that comes back.

28
00:03:51,000 --> 00:04:00,000
If we go back to the browser, you can see that messages that we have been sending in repeater are appearing in the UI.

29
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,000
And this is because we are injecting into the live WebSocket that the browser has connected with the server.

30
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:14,000
If we type another message into the browser UI, we can see the resulting messages appearing in Burp repeater.

31
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:21,000
You can also control the WebSocket connection in which messages are sent.

32
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:30,000
If we click on this pencil icon at the top of the repeater UI, here we can see a list of all of the WebSockets that have been created in this session of Burp,

33
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:34,000
which tool in Burp created them and whether they're still open.

34
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:40,000
You can select any open connection and click attach to attach to that connection.

35
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:43,000
This can be useful if you've become disconnected.

36
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:50,000
You can use your browser in the normal way to re-establish a new WebSocket connection and then you can attach into it directly from repeater.

37
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:55,000
You can also clone an open connection and create a new one that's just like it.

38
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Or you can select any closed connection and click on reconnect.

39
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:09,000
In both cases, Burp lets you reconfigure the negotiation request that was used to create this WebSocket.

40
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:15,000
So here we've got full edit access to the details of the request that will create the WebSocket.

41
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:19,000
This capability is unique to Burp Suite and it's very powerful.

42
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:28,000
There are often interesting bugs to be found by manipulating the request in the negotiation handshake before the WebSocket is opened.

43
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:33,000
Have a play with some of the labs in the Web Security Academy for examples of this.

44
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:41,000
You can disconnect or reconnect the socket that you're working on using this switch next to the pencil at the top of the UI.

45
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:54,000
And you can create a brand new WebSocket tab and repeater by clicking on the three dots in the tab header, selecting WebSocket, and then selecting the WebSocket that you want to attach to.

46
00:05:54,000 --> 00:06:01,000
So that's how to test WebSocket with Burp Suite and all of the different functions that can support your testing.

47
